::Q1::For a function is_positive(number) that returns number > 0, which input is a boundary case explicitly worth testing?
{ 
~-1000
~5
=0
~100
}

::Q2::What is the purpose of the optional message in an assert statement? 
{
~To make the assertion execute twice
=To provide additional information when the assertion fails
~To convert an AssertionError into a SyntaxError
~To prevent the condition from being evaluated
}

::Q3::Why should a newly reported bug usually get a test case that reproduces it? 
{
=The test can confirm the bug before the fix and prevent the same bug from silently returning later
~The test removes the need to fix the underlying code
~The test guarantees that no other bugs exist
~The test permanently documents that the bug must remain
}

::Q4::Which assertion correctly verifies that a 25 percent discount on 200 produces 150?
{
=assert apply_discount(200, 25) \=\= 150
~assert apply_discount(200, 25) \=\= 225
~assert apply_discount(200, 25) \=\= 50
~assert apply_discount(200, 25) \=\= 175
}

::Q5::Why can grouping several related test cases in one test function improve organization? 
{
~It makes edge cases unnecessary
~It converts all tests into integration tests
~It prevents every assertion from being evaluated
=The related behaviors for the same unit can be read and run together
}